A||||9||Mattsfiero||Stering rack||||7||
Z||000000||Mattsfiero||06-08-2000||10:02 PM||||OK Now I want to replace the steering rack...At first I thought it was a shock gone bad but after looking it over the bushings in my Rack and Pinion are shot...It looks like a relitively easy job but is there anything I should be weary of?||||reg||7
Z||000001||batboy||06-08-2000||10:40 PM||||If it's just the passenger side bushing, Rodney Dickman has a metal one to replace the nylon original one that can be installed without taking the rack off.||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||06-08-2000||11:22 PM||||I recommend you replace the rack with a remanufactured unit.<P>I just finished going through two 86 SE racks. I figured I'd rebuild them. Heck, they're simple. I measured, mic'd, leaded, tugged and pulled everything I could.<P>While the inner tie-rods were shot (passenger side far more than the driver's), both had bushings on the driver's side that were at the same clearance as Rodney's new bushing. The driver's side bushing is loose by default so the load spring/rack bearing  can press the rack rod/gears against the pinion gear. I agree that the bearings do wear though.<P>What was most telling was that the mesh between the rack gear teeth and pinion gear teeth was/were bad on both. Neither could be used. I reassembled the racks and no matter what I did, the play remained unless I cranked the load spring down to the point that you could hardly turn the pinion and it felt like two pieces of sandpaper being rubbed together. They felt terrible. If I torqued them to spec, they rattled.<P>Conclusion? Buy a remanufactured rack. The difference in the feel and steering is amazing. Even GM doesn't allow for servicing the rack rod and pinion gear seperately. These are critical to the feel and tightness of the rack. <P>I know, they run about $200. Not cheap for sure. Wait for a Carparts 33% discount coupon to show up and get one for around $155. Or buy one from Canada. The exchange rate is killer right now.<P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000003||2birds||06-08-2000||11:44 PM||||I priced mine at around $250 at Advance Auto, since mine is a GT with 3:1 ratio.  3.5:1 is less than 1/2 that.  Both are with a separate core charge.  Does this come with the inner tie rods?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rodney||06-09-2000||09:11 AM||||One thing to remember on rebuilt racks: The rebuilders can not get new racks or pinions. If they are worn beyond a certain point they are tossed. You can not buy a rebuilt rack assembly that has a new rack and pinion gear in it. The main reason the rack wears is the passenger side bushing goes bad. The extra slop allows it to load the rack against the pinion gear causing wear. Mostly in the center of the rack. <P>Rodney Dickman||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||06-09-2000||11:48 AM||||Hi Rodney, that might explain the cost of rebuilt racks being so high, especially for a manual rack.<P>And yes, the racks come with the new inner tie-rods. No outer tie-rods.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||06-09-2000||02:10 PM||||Autozone, $114 plus a core charge ($50?) for either the sport (3 turns lock to lock) or standard (3.5 turns lock to lock) rack.  In stock in Wabash, IN.  (Don't ask me why.  Only 2 others with Fieros in town.  I know them both and neither is/was getting a new rack.)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Raydar||06-09-2000||02:50 PM||||Just *try* to find an 88 rack.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>Nobody's got 'em. Just the Fiero Store.<BR>Anybody know of anything that will interchange? Anything even close?<P>Rodney, Ya' wanna sell that one with all the funny pipes all over it?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 06-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||terryk||06-09-2000||08:31 PM||||Hi Monkeyman,<P>I just called Autozone here in California and it's $114 + $50 core. Now the question is, how good are they......<P>I checked on an 86 3 turn.<P>They are out of stock, but they said they could have them in a week.<P>Anyone had any experience in a reman company called "OEM"? Yep, that's the name on the box. Reman in the USA using domestic and  extraterrestrial parts.<P>I figured I would get one and check it out. It's almost half everyone else and that many times means half the quality. Lifetime warranty.<P>"Money back if not completely satisfied". Well, we are completely satisfied with your money so we aren't giving it back.....<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 06-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||2birds||06-09-2000||09:16 PM||||Thanks for the tip, Monkeyman.  My nearest Autozone is in Brunswick, so I'll have to check there the next time I head up there.  Wrenchead and Carparts.com are more than double that price for A1 Cardone parts.  I was less than impressed with the first reman caliper I got from that brand.  My car wants to follow every rut in the road, and the only that's ever been touched in the steering is one of the inner tie rods and the stabilizer about 8 years ago.||||reg||1
